<section>
    <h2>About GI 7.025</h2>

    <p>
        This section is a study aid covering important subjects associated
        with heavy equipment operator and rigger testing and certification.
    </p>

    <div class="warning">
        <strong>Important:</strong>
        This website is for study and reference purposes only.
        Always use the current approved Saudi Aramco requirements,
        applicable regulations, manufacturer instructions, approved lift
        plans and competent-person verification for actual lifting work.
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>1. Rigger Responsibilities</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Understand the lifting operation before starting.</li>
        <li>Verify the load information provided for the lift.</li>
        <li>Select suitable lifting accessories.</li>
        <li>Inspect rigging equipment before use.</li>
        <li>Understand sling configurations and lifting angles.</li>
        <li>Protect slings from sharp edges.</li>
        <li>Ensure loads are properly connected.</li>
        <li>Maintain safe communication with the crane operator.</li>
        <li>Keep personnel away from suspended loads.</li>
        <li>Stop the operation if an unsafe condition develops.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>2. Load Weight</h2>

    <p>
        Correct load weight is fundamental to safe lifting.
        The rigger should understand the difference between mass,
        weight and the total lifted load.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
        Weight = Mass × Gravity
        <br>
        W = m × g
    </div>

    <p>
        Where:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>W</strong> = weight in Newtons</li>
        <li><strong>m</strong> = mass in kilograms</li>
        <li><strong>g</strong> = gravitational acceleration</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="important">
        In practical lifting work, use the approved load weight and
        engineering information specified for the lift rather than
        relying only on an estimated calculation.
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>3. Working Load Limit / Safe Working Load</h2>

    <p>
        Lifting accessories are normally identified with a rated
        capacity such as WLL or SWL.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
        The lifting system is limited by its weakest rated component.
    </div>

    <p>
        For example, if a lifting arrangement contains a sling,
        shackle and hook, the complete arrangement cannot safely be
        treated as having a capacity greater than the applicable
        limiting component.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>4. Sling Angle</h2>

    <p>
        Sling angle has a major effect on sling-leg tension.
        As the angle becomes smaller relative to the horizontal,
        sling tension increases.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
        T = W ÷ (2 × sin θ)
    </div>

    <p>
        This simplified equation applies to a symmetrical two-leg
        arrangement under the assumptions of the model.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>T</strong> = tension in each sling leg</li>
        <li><strong>W</strong> = load weight</li>
        <li><strong>θ</strong> = sling angle measured from the horizontal</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
        Actual sling capacity must be verified against the applicable
        sling manufacturer's rated capacities, configuration,
        connection method, angle and applicable requirements.
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>5. Sling Configurations</h2>

    <table>
        <tr>
            <th>Configuration</th>
            <th>Study Point</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Vertical</td>
            <td>Load carried directly by the sling arrangement.</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Basket</td>
            <td>Capacity depends on configuration, angle and manufacturer's rating.</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Choker</td>
            <td>Capacity is affected by choke angle, hardware and manufacturer requirements.</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Two-leg bridle</td>
            <td>Load sharing depends on geometry and center of gravity.</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Multi-leg arrangement</td>
            <td>Requires careful assessment of load distribution and geometry.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>6. D/d Ratio</h2>

    <p>
        The D/d ratio is important when a sling bends around a support
        or fitting.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
        D/d = D ÷ d
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>D</strong> = diameter of curvature around which the sling body is bent</li>
        <li><strong>d</strong> = diameter of the sling body</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        The applicable rated capacity must be determined using the
        relevant manufacturer's data and applicable requirements.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>7. Rigging Hardware</h2>

    <h3>Shackles</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Check identification and rated capacity.</li>
        <li>Inspect body and pin.</li>
        <li>Check for deformation, cracks and excessive wear.</li>
        <li>Use the correct configuration.</li>
        <li>Do not exceed the applicable rated capacity.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Hooks</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Inspect the hook before use.</li>
        <li>Check for deformation and cracks.</li>
        <li>Check hook throat opening.</li>
        <li>Verify safety latch condition where applicable.</li>
        <li>Ensure the load is properly seated in the hook.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Master Links</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Verify identification and rating.</li>
        <li>Inspect for distortion and damage.</li>
        <li>Ensure compatible connections are used.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>8. Lifting Accessories Inspection</h2>

    <p>Before use, examine lifting accessories for defects such as:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Broken wires</li>
        <li>Severe abrasion</li>
        <li>Cuts</li>
        <li>Burn damage</li>
        <li>Corrosion</li>
        <li>Distortion</li>
        <li>Cracks</li>
        <li>Damaged fittings</li>
        <li>Missing identification</li>
        <li>Damaged hooks or latches</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
        Damaged or questionable lifting equipment should not be used.
        Follow the applicable inspection, quarantine and replacement
        procedure.
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>9. Sling Protection</h2>

    <p>
        Sharp edges can damage synthetic and wire rope slings.
        Suitable protection should be used where required.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Identify sharp edges before lifting.</li>
        <li>Use suitable edge protection.</li>
        <li>Prevent uncontrolled movement of the sling.</li>
        <li>Keep slings away from crushing points.</li>
        <li>Follow manufacturer requirements.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>10. Center of Gravity</h2>

    <p>
        Understanding the center of gravity helps determine how a load
        will behave when lifted.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
        x = Σ(W × x) ÷ ΣW
    </div>

    <p>
        A load may tilt or rotate if the lifting points are not properly
        related to its center of gravity.
    </p>

    <div class="important">
        Before the main lift, the approved lifting arrangement should
        account for load geometry, center of gravity and expected load
        behavior.
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>11. Load Control</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Use suitable tag lines where required.</li>
        <li>Keep hands away from pinch points.</li>
        <li>Do not stand under suspended loads.</li>
        <li>Control unwanted rotation.</li>
        <li>Prevent personnel from entering danger zones.</li>
        <li>Use controlled crane movements.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>12. Communication</h2>

    <p>
        Clear communication between the rigger, signaller and crane
        operator is essential.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Use agreed hand signals.</li>
        <li>Use radio communication when required.</li>
        <li>Maintain clear communication during critical movements.</li>
        <li>Use one designated signaller where required by the lift procedure.</li>
        <li>If communication is lost or unclear, stop the movement.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>13. Suspended Load Safety</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Never position personnel beneath suspended loads.</li>
        <li>Establish appropriate exclusion zones.</li>
        <li>Control access to the lifting area.</li>
        <li>Consider swing and rotation.</li>
        <li>Consider dropped-object hazards.</li>
        <li>Follow the approved lift plan.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>14. Critical Lift Awareness</h2>

    <p>
        Critical lifts require additional planning and controls according
        to the applicable site and project requirements.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Confirm load weight.</li>
        <li>Confirm crane selection.</li>
        <li>Confirm lifting accessories.</li>
        <li>Review crane configuration.</li>
        <li>Review load radius.</li>
        <li>Review ground conditions.</li>
        <li>Review environmental conditions.</li>
        <li>Confirm communication arrangements.</li>
        <li>Confirm personnel responsibilities.</li>
        <li>Conduct the required pre-lift meeting.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

